```

P.S. It will buffer full collection as far as I remember, which may be not desired behavior.

.ToLookup(...) is better than .GroupBy(...).ToDictionary(...). More efficient is:

```cs
foreach (var value in source)
{
    (predicate(value) ? trueBuilder : falseBuilder).Add(value);
}
```

@jnm2 good point!

To sum up, currently there are 2 solutions for the Match problem:
1) copy (cache) elements into new collections (.ToLookup, 3rd code chunk in @simon-curtis message),
2) run 2 iterators over original IEnumerable (for example, if IEnumerable created from a file) (2nd code chunk in @simon-curtis message)

```C#
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public static class MatchExtension {
    public static (IEnumerable<T>, IEnumerable<T>) MatchBuffered<T>(this IEnumerable<T> c, Func<T, bool> cond) {
        var l = c.ToLookup(cond);
        return (l[true], l[false]);
    }
	
    public static (IEnumerable<T>, IEnumerable<T>) Match<T>(this IEnumerable<T> c, Func<T, bool> cond) {
        return (c.Where(cond), c.Where(v => !cond(v)));
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var c = new [] {"a","b","a"};
        {
            var (matched, unmatched) = c.Match(k => k=="a");
            Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",",matched));
            Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",",unmatched));
        }
        {
            var (matched, unmatched) = c.MatchBuffered(k => k=="a");
            Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",",matched));
            Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",",unmatched));
        }
    }
}
```

The non-buffered approach can be extended for IQueryable, but would require a bit code for original Expression negation.

@jnm2 you dispose data while enumerating (and with IQueryable you query each half separately without loading any data from another half).

If source IEnumerable sources data from file or network, then your IEnumerable size is not constrained by RAM capacity.

While buffered IEnumerable is simpler it has its limitations, like RAM constraints.

Ok so something like this then:

```csharp
public static class Enumerable
{
    public static (IReadOnlyList<TSource> Satisfied, IReadOnlyList<TSource> Falsified) Partition<TSource>(this IEnumerable<TSource> source, Func<TSource, bool> predicate)
    {
        ImmutableArray<TSource>.Builder satisfied = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<TSource>();
        ImmutableArray<TSource>.Builder falsified = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<TSource>();

        foreach (TSource value in source)
        {
            (predicate(value) ? satisfied : falsified).Add(value);
        }

        return (satisfied.ToImmutable(), falsified.ToImmutable());
    }
}
```
If folks agree with this signature I will update the OP and mark as ready for review.

My understanding is that there are not so many ways this could be done. Returning lazy enumerables has issues as discussed above, and you lose the analogy to GroupBy groupings which are also not lazy enumerables as mentioned above. The syntactic simplicity *is* the request, because this is not fun:

```cs
var usersByActivityStatus = usersEnumerable.GroupBy(user => user.IsActive);
var activeUsers = (usersByActivityStatus.SingleOrDefault(group => group.Key) ?? Enumerable.Empty<User>()).ToList();
var inactiveUsers = (usersByActivityStatus.SingleOrDefault(group => !group.Key) ?? Enumerable.Empty<User>()).ToList();

// Loop multiple times over, or index into, activeUsers and inactiveUsers
```

Nor is this:

```cs
var usersByActivityStatus = usersEnumerable.ToLookup(user => user.IsActive);
var activeUsers = usersByActivityStatus[true].ToList();
var inactiveUsers = usersByActivityStatus[false].ToList();

// Loop multiple times over, or index into, activeUsers and inactiveUsers
```

Nor is this:

```cs
var activeUsers = usersEnumerable.Where(user => user.IsActive).ToList();
var inactiveUsers = usersEnumerable.Where(user => !user.IsActive).ToList();

// Loop multiple times over, or index into, activeUsers and inactiveUsers
```

Often the predicate is more complex than `user => user.IsActive`. Usually it's complex enough that deduplicating the predicate logic is desirable, and that complicates the final example even more than I showed here.

This is not rare or contrived. We run into this organically. I think others could benefit from this syntax:

```cs
var (activeUsers, inactiveUsers) = usersEnumerable.Partition(group => group.Key);

// Loop multiple times over, or index into, activeUsers and inactiveUsers
```

Finally, the name came from Rust's [`Iterator::partition`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.partition) which does the same thing. This is evidence that some people have found real benefit in the concept.

@jnm2 

```C#
var usersByActivityStatus = usersEnumerable.GroupBy(user => user.IsActive);
```

Is equivalent to 

```C#
var (activeUsers, inactiveUsers) = usersEnumerable.Partition(group => group.Key);
```

The only difference being is the return type structure. One being two collections and one being a single key collection.
I dont see how:

```C#
var usersByActivityStatus = usersEnumerable.GroupBy(user => user.IsActive);
var activeUsers = usersByActivityStatus[true].ToList();
var inactiveUsers = usersByActivityStatus[false].ToList();
```

Is any different from

```C#
var (activeUsers, inactiveUsers) = usersEnumerable.Partition(group => group.Key);
var activeUsersCollection = activeUsers.ToList();
var inactiveUsersCollection = inactiveUsers.ToList();
```

Other than the fact you have to do a hash table lookup between two values of true and false (which is trivial).

The only way it would be different is if it was deferred which like I said above, doesnt provide the performance you expect as you would have to enumerate the entire collection twice on both `satisfied` and `unsatisfied` together.

Maybe I see an argument to put it on a concrete collection type such as `List<T>`, but `IEnumerable<T>` has too many side effects to even consider (if deferring), IMO. 

Edit: Just because a language has a feature, doesn't mean its evidence for a good one. There are plenty of things in C# I wouldn't want to see other languages adopt.

Would I be right in saying this would be the implementation?

```C#
public static class Extensions
{
    public static (IReadOnlyList<TSource> Satisfied, IReadOnlyList<TSource> Falsified) Partition<TSource>(
        this IEnumerable<TSource> source, Func<TSource, bool> predicate)
    {
        var results = source.ToLookup(predicate.Invoke);
        return (results[true].ToList(), results[false].ToList());
    }
}
```

